			"Message": "fix(Cocoon): Route onDidExecuteCommand through Emitter replacing dead Tauri listen\n\nThe prior `onDidExecuteCommand` implementation called `import(\"@tauri-apps/api/event\").listen(\"sky://commands/executed\", ...)` which never fires in Node.js (no `window.__TAURI__`), so every extension hooking this event silently missed all command executions. Replace with a subscription to the `commands.executed` Emitter channel, fed by two sources:\n\n- `$acceptCommandExecuted` case in Notification/Handler.ts catches Mountain's Vine dual-emit after every Mountain-routed `commands:execute` round-trip and forwards to the Emitter channel.\n- Local command routes in Commands/Namespace.ts emit on the same channel (symmetric with the Mountain branch) so extension-to-extension `executeCommand` calls are visible too.\n\nAlso extend the `viewId` dual-key resolution strategy (established in the prior commit for `setHtml`/`postMessage`) to five more webview mutators in CreateWebviewPanel.ts: `dispose`, `setTitle`, `setIconPath`, `setOptions`, and `reveal`. These operations can fire before the workbench finishes mounting the panel's IOverlayWebview; the `viewId` fallback in Sky's `InstallWebview.ts` ensures they still reach their target.\n\nMark Terminal/Service.ts as deprecated — its wire calls reference non-existent Mountain handler names and it is imported only by a dead bootstrap path. The active terminal management lives in `CreateTerminal.ts`. Retained pending vertical-split cleanup (Mountain-Crate-Split.md §#7a).",
			"SHA": "fcb3f91e7c485985bab07b6e1da67c20b687531f"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-05-25T21:38:14Z",
			"File": [
				"⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				1181,
				2
			],
			"Message": "fix(Cocoon): Send viewId in webview setHtml and postMessage to fix panel first-paint race\n\nThe `html` setter and `postMessage` method on WebviewPanel only sent `handle` to Mountain, relying on Sky's per-handle WebviewInput binding to route the payload. During first-paint, the `webview.create` ack can arrive before the workbench's `IOverlayWebview` instantiation has bound the handle to a WebviewInput, causing the initial setHtml to land in `ParkedHtml` and postMessage to miss its target.\n\nAdd `viewId: ViewType` to both `webview.setHtml` and `webview.postMessage` request payloads, matching the pattern already established in `CreateWebviewViewBuilder.ts:268`. Sky's `InstallWebview.ts` resolution path (lines 56-57, 300-302) accepts either `handle` or `viewId`; sending both eliminates the race by providing a fallback viewType-keyed lookup.\n\nAlso deprecate the `MountainGRPCClientLayer` and its Effect-TS service tag with documentation pointing consumers to the canonical wire-method names (`Window.ShowMessage`, `webview.setHtml`, `secrets.get`, etc.). The Layer is exported but never provided in any `Bootstrap.ts` entry point, so Effect branches referencing the tag fail at the dependency layer before any gRPC call fires. Removal scheduled for Track-B vertical split work.",
			"SHA": "82e92eaf8ba75052d8b1434e9b18b63b36235253"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-05-25T21:28:39Z",
			"File": [
				"⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				1181,
				1
			],
			"Message": "fix(Cocoon): Skip failure count when circuit breaker is already Open\n\nOnce the circuit breaker trips to Open, in-flight gRPC requests that were launched before the trip can still resolve with errors. The original code incremented `circuitBreakerFailureCount` unconditionally on every error, causing the counter to pile up beyond the intended threshold (observed at N≥19 in the 2026-05-25 panel-debug run) while the breaker was already Open and doing nothing useful — every such increment produced a noisy `[Breaker] transition from=OPEN to=Open failures=N` dev-log line.\n\nAdd an early return in `CheckCircuitBreaker` when the state is already `Open` so that post-trip completions are logged at debug level but not counted. Recovery remains driven by the existing timeout-based transition to HalfOpen in the caller path, not by piling on more failures.\n\nThis eliminates the dev-log chatter and keeps the failure counter accurate for the Closed/HalfOpen windows that actually drive breaker state transitions.",
			"SHA": "8fa4cdb83c199b1859c44b151f14ae6c1194dd7f"
		},
		{
